    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass, including the new `coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` acceptance test.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-registration cap (reject with **429** once 100+ owners have been registered today, by `registrationDate`), following the existing `CityCapacityValidator` pattern so each piece of logic lives where it belongs:\n\n1. **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 added `countOwnersRegisteredOn(LocalDate date)`, mirroring the existing `countOwnersInCity` (a read-only stream filter over owners). Counting lives in the service layer alongside the analogous city count.\n\n2. **`DailyRegistrationLimitExceededException`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` documented as mapping to `429 Too Many Requests`, matching the style of `CityCapacityExceededException` / `DuplicateTelephoneException`.\n\n3. **`DailyRegistrationLimitValidator`** (new `@Component`) \u2014 holds the `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100` constant and throws the exception when today's count has reached the limit, mirroring `CityCapacityValidator`.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 injected the validator and invoke `dailyRegistrationLimitValidator.validate()` alongside the other pre-save guards.\n\n5. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` mapping the new exception to `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429), consistent with the existing handlers.\n\nThe candidate owner's `registrationDate` defaults to `LocalDate.now()` on persist, so counting existing owners with today's date correctly reflects \"created today.\"",
